    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> wrote for new rulers rather than hereditary ones, and set aside the classical tradition's concern with ideal regimes in favour of the effectual truth of how men live.</li><li>He wrote it in 1513, a few months after the Medici regime had arrested, tortured and banished him, and meant it for the Medici; it was printed only in 1532, five years after his death.</li><li>Its advice on cruelty, deceit and the keeping of one's word shocked contemporaries, put it on the Index in 1559, and left a lasting mark on the words Machiavellian and politics.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>, his extensive written comments on The Prince were found in the emperor's coach and taken by the Prussian military.</p></aside>
    </section>

